WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:06.706
"Randomized Controlled Trials of Taurine
Supplementation in Humans"

00:00:06.853 --> 00:00:09.760
We know taurine abundance
decreases during aging,

00:00:09.760 --> 00:00:11.680
and a reversal
of this decline

00:00:11.680 --> 00:00:12.880
through taurine supplementation

00:00:12.880 --> 00:00:14.560
increases the healthspan
and lifespan

00:00:14.560 --> 00:00:15.760
in mice and worms,

00:00:15.760 --> 00:00:17.680
and health in monkeys.

00:00:17.680 --> 00:00:19.840
So, this means
taurine deficiency

00:00:19.840 --> 00:00:23.440
is a driver of aging
in these species.

00:00:23.440 --> 00:00:24.960
To test
whether taurine deficiency

00:00:24.960 --> 00:00:28.080
is a driver of aging
in humans too,

00:00:28.080 --> 00:00:30.720
long-term, well-controlled
taurine supplementation trials

00:00:30.720 --> 00:00:32.880
that measure human
healthspan and lifespan

00:00:32.880 --> 00:00:35.760
as outcomes are required.

00:00:35.760 --> 00:00:39.520
Why haven't
these happened already?

00:00:39.520 --> 00:00:41.120
You know why.

00:00:41.120 --> 00:00:42.880
As a naturally
occurring substance,

00:00:42.880 --> 00:00:46.640
taurine cannot be exploited
through a commercial patent.

00:00:46.640 --> 00:00:49.280
You can buy pure taurine powder,

00:00:49.280 --> 00:00:53.280
literally by the bucket,
at hardly a penny per gram.

00:00:53.280 --> 00:00:55.760
So, who's going
to fund studies on it?

00:00:55.760 --> 00:00:58.400
In population studies,
higher blood taurine levels

00:00:58.400 --> 00:00:59.680
are associated with less obesity

00:00:59.680 --> 00:01:02.080
and a lower prevalence
of type 2 diabetes,

00:01:02.080 --> 00:01:04.320
but, when put to test,

00:01:04.320 --> 00:01:06.400
as reviewed in
this 2020 meta-analysis

00:01:06.400 --> 00:01:07.920
of randomized controlled trials,

00:01:07.920 --> 00:01:10.480
taurine supplementation
had no significant effect

00:01:10.480 --> 00:01:13.120
on blood sugar control,
LDL cholesterol,

00:01:13.120 --> 00:01:14.160
or body weight,

00:01:14.160 --> 00:01:15.520
though it did
drop blood pressures

00:01:15.520 --> 00:01:17.200
and triglycerides a few points,

00:01:17.200 --> 00:01:19.520
at a daily dose of
half a gram to six grams a day

00:01:19.520 --> 00:01:21.440
between 15 days and 6 months.

00:01:21.440 --> 00:01:24.480
A 2024 systematic review
and meta-analysis

00:01:24.480 --> 00:01:26.080
of randomized controlled trials

00:01:26.080 --> 00:01:27.920
found the same
small blood pressure

00:01:27.920 --> 00:01:29.360
and triglycerides benefit,

00:01:29.360 --> 00:01:31.280
as well as a small,
six-point drop

00:01:31.280 --> 00:01:34.080
in fasting blood sugars...

00:01:34.080 --> 00:01:37.040
though none of those few trials
on people with diabetes

00:01:37.040 --> 00:01:38.800
showed that
blood pressure benefit,

00:01:38.800 --> 00:01:43.040
though it did help
their blood sugars.

00:01:43.040 --> 00:01:45.120
Taurine can also slow
our heart rate

00:01:45.120 --> 00:01:46.560
by three or four
beats per minute,

00:01:46.560 --> 00:01:47.920
which is considered
a good thing,

00:01:47.920 --> 00:01:50.080
and, in addition
to lowering blood pressures,

00:01:50.080 --> 00:01:52.000
it can improve
heart muscle function

00:01:52.000 --> 00:01:54.160
in patients with heart failure.

00:01:54.160 --> 00:01:56.000
What about artery function?

00:01:56.000 --> 00:01:57.440
People with type 1 diabetes

00:01:57.440 --> 00:01:59.200
were given
500 milligrams of taurine

00:01:59.200 --> 00:02:00.720
three times a day for two weeks,

00:02:00.720 --> 00:02:03.040
and researchers saw a
significant improvement

00:02:03.040 --> 00:02:04.880
in their artery function.

00:02:04.880 --> 00:02:07.200
As you can see,
those with type 1 diabetes

00:02:07.200 --> 00:02:08.960
start out
with reduced artery function

00:02:08.960 --> 00:02:10.560
compared to healthy controls,

00:02:10.560 --> 00:02:13.200
and giving them a placebo
doesn't seem to help.

00:02:13.200 --> 00:02:14.960
But the taurine
seemed to elevate

00:02:14.960 --> 00:02:18.880
their artery function
closer to healthy levels.

00:02:18.880 --> 00:02:20.240
What about
improving artery function

00:02:20.240 --> 00:02:22.480
in people without diabetes?

00:02:22.480 --> 00:02:25.600
In this study of individuals
with prehypertension,

00:02:25.600 --> 00:02:27.280
about 130 over 80,

00:02:27.280 --> 00:02:28.640
who took about the same amount,

00:02:28.640 --> 00:02:30.640
1.6 grams a day for 12 weeks,

00:02:30.640 --> 00:02:32.880
they got the same
blood pressure benefit

00:02:32.880 --> 00:02:33.920
seen in the other trials

00:02:33.920 --> 00:02:37.040
and, importantly,
improvement in artery function

00:02:37.040 --> 00:02:38.640
compared to placebo.

00:02:38.640 --> 00:02:41.680
What about effects on
inflammation and oxidation?

00:02:41.680 --> 00:02:44.320
Taurine can decrease the levels
of c-reactive protein,

00:02:44.320 --> 00:02:46.160
a marker of oxidative stress,

00:02:46.160 --> 00:02:47.520
though it doesn't appear
to have much effect

00:02:47.520 --> 00:02:50.160
on some other markers
of inflammation.

00:02:50.160 --> 00:02:52.960
So, okay, some modest effects,

00:02:52.960 --> 00:02:55.040
but there is
a substantial mismatch

00:02:55.040 --> 00:02:57.040
between the strong
scientific foundation

00:02:57.040 --> 00:02:58.480
supportive
of the health benefits

00:02:58.480 --> 00:03:01.120
of taurine
in animal studies,

00:03:01.120 --> 00:03:02.480
and the quality
of clinical trials

00:03:02.480 --> 00:03:04.400
and their outcomes
in assessing the efficacy

00:03:04.400 --> 00:03:06.880
of taurine in human health.

00:03:06.880 --> 00:03:09.200
What's the harm
in taking it, though?

00:03:09.200 --> 00:03:10.800
It's cheap and easy to find.

00:03:10.800 --> 00:03:14.160
A one-gram dose would cost
as little as a penny a day.

00:03:14.160 --> 00:03:16.560
Most Americans are getting
about 150 milligrams a day

00:03:16.560 --> 00:03:18.400
in their diet,
mostly from meat.

00:03:18.400 --> 00:03:19.680
We make taurine ourselves,

00:03:19.680 --> 00:03:22.000
and so do many
of the animals we eat.

00:03:22.000 --> 00:03:23.280
In fact,
our realization

00:03:23.280 --> 00:03:25.520
that we made so much on our own

00:03:25.520 --> 00:03:27.760
came from studies of vegans
who had taurine levels

00:03:27.760 --> 00:03:29.200
that were not
significantly different

00:03:29.200 --> 00:03:30.800
than those eating meat,

00:03:30.800 --> 00:03:33.600
though they were
about 15 percent lower.

00:03:33.600 --> 00:03:36.000
A subsequent study found
the taurine levels of vegans

00:03:36.000 --> 00:03:38.480
were more like 20 percent
lower than meat eaters,

00:03:38.480 --> 00:03:41.680
which did reach
statistical significance.

00:03:41.680 --> 00:03:43.440
The clinical implications

00:03:43.440 --> 00:03:47.280
of reduced blood taurine levels
in vegans are not known.

00:03:47.280 --> 00:03:49.680
Our ability to make taurine
is supported by the fact

00:03:49.680 --> 00:03:50.800
that taurine deficiency

00:03:50.800 --> 00:03:52.800
has never been found
in vegetarians,

00:03:52.800 --> 00:03:55.120
but just because
vegan levels aren't deficient

00:03:55.120 --> 00:03:58.080
doesn't mean they are
necessarily optimal.

00:03:58.080 --> 00:04:00.560
Taurine is considered
nonessential in adults

00:04:00.560 --> 00:04:02.400
because it can be
readily synthesized

00:04:02.400 --> 00:04:03.440
within our own bodies,

00:04:03.440 --> 00:04:05.600
though infants may need
to get it in breast milk

00:04:05.600 --> 00:04:08.560
until they can ramp up
their own synthesis.

00:04:08.560 --> 00:04:11.440
But can adults make enough
for optimal health,

00:04:11.440 --> 00:04:15.920
especially as we get older
and all our levels decline?

00:04:15.920 --> 00:04:18.880
Why do our levels
even decline as we age?

00:04:18.880 --> 00:04:20.160
It's not just because

00:04:20.160 --> 00:04:21.920
we may be getting
less in our diet,

00:04:21.920 --> 00:04:24.320
since if you get
none in your diet,

00:04:24.320 --> 00:04:26.240
your levels may only be
about 20 percent lower

00:04:26.240 --> 00:04:29.600
but levels over a lifespan
decline by about 80 percent,

00:04:29.600 --> 00:04:34.000
suggesting we lose some
of our capacity to churn it out.

00:04:34.000 --> 00:04:37.600
Taurine has been envisioned
as a longevity vitamin,

00:04:37.600 --> 00:04:40.560
meaning it isn't like
an actual vitamin-vitamin,

00:04:40.560 --> 00:04:42.560
where we die
if we don't get enough.

00:04:42.560 --> 00:04:44.000
But even though we make it,

00:04:44.000 --> 00:04:46.400
we may also need
to get some in our diet

00:04:46.400 --> 00:04:48.880
to optimize metabolism.

00:04:48.880 --> 00:04:50.560
In a review of what clinicians

00:04:50.560 --> 00:04:52.160
might want to discuss
with their patients

00:04:52.160 --> 00:04:53.280
on a plant-based diet

00:04:53.280 --> 00:04:54.960
who are suffering
from some kind of problem,

00:04:54.960 --> 00:04:57.680
are these conditionally
essential nutrients,

00:04:57.680 --> 00:04:59.120
where,
under normal conditions,

00:04:59.120 --> 00:05:00.640
they can be produced
in our own body

00:05:00.640 --> 00:05:01.680
in sufficient amounts,

00:05:01.680 --> 00:05:03.760
but something may interfere
with synthesis,

00:05:03.760 --> 00:05:06.880
or conditions may arise
where we may need more.

00:05:06.880 --> 00:05:08.960
Taurine is included
in this list,

00:05:08.960 --> 00:05:10.960
as is carnitine,
another compound

00:05:10.960 --> 00:05:13.840
found in meat
that we can make on our own.

00:05:13.840 --> 00:05:15.360
But imagine if people

00:05:15.360 --> 00:05:17.600
started taking
carnitine supplements,

00:05:17.600 --> 00:05:20.880
thinking, "Why not,
what harm can it do?"

00:05:20.880 --> 00:05:22.400
Of course, we now know
that carnitine

00:05:22.400 --> 00:05:24.560
is one of the things
we try to avoid in meat,

00:05:24.560 --> 00:05:26.320
because it turns into TMAO,

00:05:26.320 --> 00:05:28.080
and may increase
the risk of heart disease,

00:05:28.080 --> 00:05:30.400
kidney disease, and many
of our leading killers.

00:05:30.400 --> 00:05:32.480
Indeed, if you give people
carnitine supplements,

00:05:32.480 --> 00:05:34.480
their TMAO levels shoot up.

00:05:34.480 --> 00:05:37.520
So, might that be
a precautionary tale

00:05:37.520 --> 00:05:39.520
when it comes to taurine?

00:05:39.520 --> 00:05:41.360
Take arginine, for example.

00:05:41.360 --> 00:05:44.320
It's another non-essential
amino acid in adults

00:05:44.320 --> 00:05:46.080
that has a variety of functions

00:05:46.080 --> 00:05:47.760
other than being
part of proteins,

00:05:47.760 --> 00:05:50.320
including delaying
the aging senescence

00:05:50.320 --> 00:05:52.720
of arterial lining cells.

00:05:52.720 --> 00:05:54.000
Animal studies have shown

00:05:54.000 --> 00:05:55.680
all sorts
of heart healthy benefits

00:05:55.680 --> 00:05:57.280
of arginine supplementation,

00:05:57.280 --> 00:05:59.040
including
improved artery function

00:05:59.040 --> 00:06:01.760
and inhibition
of atherosclerotic plaque,

00:06:01.760 --> 00:06:03.440
just like taurine.

00:06:03.440 --> 00:06:06.000
So, reviews like
these suggest studies

00:06:06.000 --> 00:06:08.640
strongly support favorable
cardiovascular effects

00:06:08.640 --> 00:06:10.800
accruing from L-arginine
supplementation

00:06:10.800 --> 00:06:14.160
based on short-term studies,
just like taurine.

00:06:14.160 --> 00:06:17.360
But long-term treatment
with arginine

00:06:17.360 --> 00:06:19.760
actually accelerates
the aging process

00:06:19.760 --> 00:06:22.800
in human artery lining cells.

00:06:22.800 --> 00:06:26.880
Did this paradoxical effect
cause any actual problems?

00:06:26.880 --> 00:06:28.160
Short-term supplementation

00:06:28.160 --> 00:06:30.160
showed an improvement
in artery function.

00:06:30.160 --> 00:06:32.720
In contrast,
long-term therapy

00:06:32.720 --> 00:06:34.240
with arginine supplementation

00:06:34.240 --> 00:06:36.640
did not improve artery
or heart function.

00:06:36.640 --> 00:06:41.394
Instead, it led
to premature death.

